Epstein-Barr virus infection
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Fever/exudative pharyngitis, Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, Tender splenomegaly, Positive heterophile antibody test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-007
Reason for Visit: Severe, progressive odynophagia and high fever causing an inability to swallow saliva or fluids, requiring urgent assessment for airway compromise and hydration status.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a young adult with a persistent high fever, massive neck swelling, and an inability to swallow fluids. Urgent physical assessment is required to rule out impending airway obstruction (e.g., peritonsillar abscess) and evaluate splenic enlargement to prevent catastrophic rupture prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My throat is entirely blocked, I can't swallow my own spit, and the glands in my neck are huge."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced gradually 2 weeks ago with profound generalized fatigue, extreme lethargy, and systemic malaise. Over the last 4 days, the patient developed a persistent high fever and severe exudative pharyngitis. The patient notes massive, symmetric, and tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy. Currently, oral intake is negligible due to severe pain. The patient was inappropriately prescribed amoxicillin by a telehealth service 3 days ago and has since developed a severe, generalized, brightly erythematous maculopapular rash.
Functional Status: Bedbound, exhausted, holding a cup to spit saliva into.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety from the parent regarding the rash and the patient's inability to eat or drink.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 105 | Temp: 38.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, marked fatigue, muffled "hot potato" voice, prominent bilateral neck swelling.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam reveals kissing tonsils covered in a thick, greyish-white shaggy exudate. No uvular deviation. Petechiae are distinctly localized strictly to the junction of the hard and soft palate. Neck exam reveals massive, tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy. Abdominal exam reveals mild tender hepatomegaly and palpable, tender splenomegaly extending 2cm below the costal margin. Diffuse maculopapular rash over the trunk and extremities. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Fever/exudative pharyngitis, Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, Tender splenomegaly, and Positive heterophile antibody test (pending).
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting in a quiet, dark room. Spit cup at bedside.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently taking amoxicillin; advised to cease immediately.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Epstein-Barr virus infection (Infectious Mononucleosis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Streptococcal Pharyngitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Posterior (rather than anterior) cervical lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and the classic amoxicillin-induced maculopapular rash.

Peritonsillar Abscess (Quinsy) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral tonsillar enlargement without unilateral soft palate swelling or uvular deviation.

Acute HIV infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of massive exudative tonsillitis, which is less common in acute retroviral syndrome, though heterophile-negative cases warrant screening.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe infectious mononucleosis complicated by near-obstructive tonsillar hypertrophy, dehydration, and a classic amoxicillin-induced rash. The muffled voice and inability to swallow saliva raised immediate red flags for airway compromise. A definitive in-person clinical examination was mandatory to secure the airway, differentiate from a surgical peritonsillar abscess, and formally evaluate the abdomen for critical splenomegaly. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of acute airway obstruction or traumatic splenic rupture, wholly justifying an urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate cessation of amoxicillin. Prescribe a short, tapering course of oral corticosteroids (e.g., prednisolone 50mg daily for 3 days) to rapidly reduce tonsillar edema and restore airway patency. Request bloods for FBC (to look for atypical lymphocytosis), LFTs, and a Monospot test.
Positive Behavioral Change: By using a local anesthetic throat spray 15 minutes prior to meals, the patient will numb the oropharynx, allowing for the successful swallowing of cold liquids; this pain relief will reinforce the habit of pre-medicating and ensure adequate hydration is maintained.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to assess airway, hydration, and review blood results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient experiences stridor (noisy breathing), absolute inability to swallow oral secretions, sudden severe left upper quadrant abdominal pain, or left shoulder tip pain (Kehr's sign indicating splenic rupture).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending serological confirmation; airway status is dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute pharyngitis and fever will subside over 7-10 days, but profound fatigue and splenomegaly may persist for weeks or months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your spleen is currently enlarged and extremely fragile. You must completely avoid all contact sports, heavy lifting, or rigorous activity for at least 4 weeks to prevent a life-threatening rupture.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The rash is a specific, known reaction to the amoxicillin combining with the virus, not a true penicillin allergy. The steroids will aggressively shrink your tonsils so you can breathe and drink safely.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking the antibiotic. Rest completely. Only consume cool, soft foods and liquids.

